  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study uses the difference-in-means refusal direction in an input-optimization attack that suppresses alignment and reports behavioral attack outcomes; this is not the original direct all-layer ablation or addition intervention.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports a layer-wise projection-ablation study in Appendix A, but says it is used to select a layer for the attack; those values are excluded under the rule for results used only for layer selection. The paper's reported detector and RTV performance is not otherwise a test of the claim versions. The Directional-GCG and JB-GCG attack results are included because they optimize against the refusal direction and report harmful-output attack success.",
    "test_reason": "The study borrows the Arditi difference-in-means refusal direction as a tool. It builds suffix attacks (Directional-GCG, JB-GCG, RTV-GCG, ML-GCG) that suppress that direction on purpose, and a detector (RTV) based on it. Its reported results are the ASR of its own attacks against JBShield and RTV, and RTV's detection performance. Every ASR in the record requires evading a detector, so it measures the defense, not whether the direction mediates refusal or whether natural jailbreaks work through it. The only direct ablation result (Appendix A, Fig. 5) is used for layer selection. The study never checks that existing jailbreaks suppress the direction, and never trains against ablation.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that JB-GCG breaks JBShield's AND-gate: it suppresses the toxic concept while the jailbreak concept stays active, reaching up to 53.4% ASR vs. JBShield-D and 30.7% vs. JBShield-M. Multi-layer refusal-direction fingerprints scored with Mahalanobis distance (RTV) detect JB-GCG at 0.99 AUROC. The strongest white-box adaptive attack on RTV reaches 7% ASR at about 13× the compute.",
    "recipe": "close",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses the original difference-in-means refusal direction, but suppresses alignment through discrete suffix optimization rather than the original direct activation ablation/addition protocol; the paper's layer-wise ablation is described as a layer-selection study.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Attack outcomes vary with direction and optimization layers, evaluation dataset, defense stage (JBShield-D versus JBShield-M), and JBShield calibration size.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Suffixes optimized to suppress refusal-direction alignment at layer 18 sit near zero there. At layer 32 they drift toward the harmful-side alignment, so their multi-layer fingerprint matches neither harmless nor harmful prompts.",
        "proposed_claim": "Input-side suppression of the refusal direction at one layer leaves an inter-layer-inconsistent refusal-direction profile.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r27",
          "r28"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Under adaptive attacks against RTV, about 95% of prompts were jailbroken by refusal-direction suppression, but few evaded the fingerprint. The authors say evasion, not refusal suppression, is the binding constraint.",
        "proposed_claim": "Suppressing the refusal direction through input optimization is cheap, but hiding that suppression across layers is hard.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "Experiments target one model, Llama-3-8B-Instruct. The reported layer-wise ablation is used for layer selection and does not establish the full all-layer-and-position intervention. No general-capability evaluation, addition intervention, or uncertainty estimates for attack rates are reported. Some fingerprint results are presented only in figures.",
    "rows_left_out": "Appendix A's layer-wise ablation scores are omitted because the paper states they are used to select the extraction layer; per the gate rule, results used only for layer selection are not rows. Detection results for RTV and other defenses are not rows because they do not test the claim versions. No other claim-relevant results were left out.",
    "untested": [],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "All experiments use one model, Llama-3-8B-Instruct. Two results come closest to the claim, and neither is a row. First, Appendix A/Fig. 5: per-layer projection ablation lowers the Llama-Guard safety score most at layers 17–22. This is consistent with V1, but figure-only, used for layer selection, and lacks addition and capability checks. Second, §7.4 reports a 95% jailbreak rate when the direction is suppressed through a suffix. Both come from the study's own deliberate attacks, not tests of naturally occurring jailbreaks (V3). Odd sign convention: harmful prompts show negative cosine with a direction defined as harmful minus harmless (Fig. 3/4), which the text does not explain."
  },
